proof_plan:
Factoring the difference of squares twice:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = 16 \AA^2
| r = \paren {2 a b - a^2 - b^2 + c^2} \paren {2 a b + a^2 + b^2 - c^2}
}}
{{eqn | r = \paren {c^2 - \paren {a - b}^2} \paren {\paren {a + b}^2 - c^2}
}}
{{eqn | r = \paren {b + c - a} \paren {a + c - b} \paren {a + b - c} \paren {a + b + c}
}}
{{end-eqn}}

The factors are $2 \paren {s - a}$, $2 \paren {s - b}$, $2 \paren {s - c}$ and $2 s$.
Hence $\AA^2 = s \paren {s - a} \paren {s - b} \paren {s - c}$, which is [[Heron's Formula]].
{{qed}}
